      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, you're asking for the actual numbers! While I can certainly help you with the math, I must warn you that even the most precise PSI calculations can be undermined by a sudden, unexpected gopher or a rogue goblin trying to sabotage your framing! In the Vancouver area, the standard **live load** for a residential deck is typically **40 pounds per square foot (psf)**. This is the weight you must design for when people, furniture, and that pesky goblin who decides to sit on your railing are all on the deck.\n\nFor your **15-foot span**, the **dead load** (the weight of the Western Red Cedar framing itself) usually ranges from **10 to 15 psf**, depending on the thickness of your joists and beams. So, your total design load would be around **50 to 55 psf**. However, Vancouver's climate adds a twist: you must also account for **snow load**, which can add another **20 to 30 psf** depending on the specific zone in the Lower Mainland. If your dead load is too high, the posts might sag under the weight of the cedar, but if your live load is underestimated,",
